export anchor PluginSources:
    description:
        A project has the plugins bundled with the compiler, and the plugins
        its piton.config.pi declares. Both are listed by `piton plugins`, and
        both add their commands to the CLI.

    bundled:
        A bundled plugin is part of the compiler. Its Piton files are a
        bundled package, @piton/ and its name, so `use @piton/test` works in
        any project, and its declaration and code are embedded with them.
        Every project has every bundled plugin, and its commands, without
        configuring anything. A bundled plugin with a build only builds for a
        project that lists its settings, so a project that never mentions
        Belay gets no agent files.

        Four are bundled, each in the compiler's source with its declaration
        in plugin.pi, its TypeScript in src, and any Piton files in pi:
        Belay, in plugins/belay, the test plugin, in plugins/test, the
        migrate plugin, in plugins/migrate, and the init plugin, in
        plugins/init, which also holds its templates in templates. Every file
        under a bundled plugin's src, and under the init plugin's templates,
        is embedded, so a file added there is part of the next build without
        being listed anywhere.

    installed:
        A project adds a plugin by listing its piton-plugin anchor under
        plugins in piton.config.pi. Its entry, and every file its code
        imports, are read from the directory of the file the declaration is
        in.

        ```piton
        \\\
        use @piton/config
        use @piton/plugin

        export piton-config App:
            root: ./spec
            plugins:
                - {Hello}

        piton-plugin Hello:
            name: hello
            entry: ./plugin/index.ts
            commands:
                hello:
                    about: Say hello
        \\\
        ```

        A plugin shared between projects is a package: its declaration, code
        and Piton files are in the package, installed into tethers/ like any
        other, and the project imports the declaration from it and lists it.
        Its Piton files are imported by the package's name, as every
        package's are.

    settings:
        A project's settings for a plugin are an anchor implementing the
        abstract anchor the plugin's declaration names as its settings,
        listed under plugins too.

        ```piton
        \\\
        export piton-config App:
            root: ./spec
            plugins:
                - {Tests}

        test-config Tests:
            agent: codex
        \\\
        ```

        For a bundled plugin, the anchor has to come from its package, so the
        test plugin's settings implement @piton/test's TestConfig. A project
        lists at most one settings anchor for each plugin; two is an error
        when the plugin reads them. The plugin checks their values itself, and
        reports a wrong one where it is written.

    listing:
        Each anchor under plugins is a plugin's declaration or a plugin's
        settings. Anything else is warned about, since nothing would read it,
        and so is anything under plugins that isn't an anchor.

    loading:
        Plugins are found every time piton runs, by reading the configuration
        of the project the working directory is inside: the nearest
        piton.config.pi in it or a directory above it. So a plugin's commands
        are there as soon as it is listed, and work from anywhere in the
        project. Finding them compiles Piton only: no plugin code runs
        until one of its commands does.

        Before a plugin's command runs, the configuration has to be free of
        errors, which are reported first, and problems with the project's
        plugins are reported as warnings. A plugin that inspects projects is
        the exception: its commands run even when the configuration has
        errors, since repairing it may be what they are for.
